@page "/finance/cash-forecast"
@page "/finance"
@page "/finance/cash-summary"
@using Jewel.JPMS.Commercial
@using Jewel.JPMS.Features.Cashflow
@using static Jewel.JPMS.Features.Cashflow.CashflowDisplay
@using Jewel.JPMS.Contracts.Projects
@using Jewel.JPMS.Contracts.Retention
@using Jewel.JPMS.Features.Commercial
@using Jewel.JPMS.Features.Procurement
@using Jewel.JPMS.Features.Projects
@inject AuthService Auth
@inject SessionService Session
@inject ProjectListReadModel Projects
@inject ProjectFinancialSummaryReadModel Summary
@inject ProjectWorkOrdersReadModel WorkOrders
@inject ValuationLinesReadModel Lines
@inject ValuationClaimsReadModel Claims
@inject ClaimLinesReadModel ClaimEntries
@inject IValuationInvoiceStore Invoices
@inject IProjectContractStore Contracts
@inject IXeroCashSummaryStore Cash
@inject ForecastOverheadsStorage OverheadsStorage
@inject IQueryClient Queries
@inject ICommandSender Commands
@inject NavigationManager Nav
@implements IDisposable

<PageTitle>Cash Forecast · JPMS</PageTitle>

@*
    The company Cash Forecast (the sidebar's Financial Reports folder) — successor to the Cash
    Summary on the same slugs (/finance and /finance/cash-summary still land here; the canonical
    route is /finance/cash-forecast).

    Two sections, one load:

    FORECAST (top) — the time axis the Cash Summary never had: every known future cash movement
    placed in the month it is expected to land (CashForecastPhasing, contracts — pure and
    unit-tested), a net movement line, and for directors the running bank balance seeded from
    Xero, with the lowest point flagged. The phasing RULES are the interim set from
    docs/Financial-Reports-and-Nav-Refactor-Plan.md §4, awaiting FD/consultant sign-off — hence
    the standing amber notice. The figures being phased are the statements' own (RowFor below),
    so each project's months sum to its Project Completion Cashflow to the penny — checked live
    in the reconciliation footer, red if it ever fails.

    POSITION TO COMPLETION (below the divider) — the former Cash Summary, unchanged: the
    combined to-completion statement and the by-project table (finance meeting 2026-08-03,
    retired page Pages/CashSummary.razor). Kept while the forecast is unconfirmed so nothing
    anyone relies on disappears; the old bank tiles are not duplicated here — their figures live
    in the KPI strip on top.

    Figures come from exactly the same sources and arithmetic as each project's Cashflow tab
    (ValuationSummaryFigures, RetentionSchedule, ProjectDrawdown, part-payment-aware unpaid
    bills), so this page and the per-project statement can never disagree.

    The Future valuations row carries the FD's two per-project knobs (2026-08-13 — "France's
    next valuation won't be till next month… Woodhouse seems to be a lot less than that…
    Abbott will probably land up being more"), edited inline on its expanded lines: WHEN the
    next valuation lands (the project's NextExpectedValuationDate) and roughly how much the
    architect is expected to certify per month (ExpectedMonthlyValuation). With a monthly
    figure set, the engine claims at that rate until left-to-claim runs out instead of
    spreading evenly to practical completion — the AMOUNT is still the statement's, only its
    timing moves, so the tie-back below stays exact. Both are stored on the project (API-gated
    Director/FD/PM), so the tweak survives reloads and every colleague sees the same forecast.
*@

<Page>
    <PageHeader>
        <SubtitleContent>
            Every known future cash movement in the month it is expected to land@(IsDirector ? ", and the bank balance that results — lowest point first" : "").
        </SubtitleContent>
    </PageHeader>

    @* Standing notice until the FD and consultant sign off the §4 phasing rules — the
       AMOUNTS are live and tie to the statements; only their TIMING is provisional. *@
    <Notice Tone="Tone.Warning" Class="mb-6 max-w-4xl">
        <span class="font-semibold">Unconfirmed figures.</span> The timing rules behind this
        forecast are awaiting sign-off (Financial-Reports-and-Nav-Refactor-Plan §4). Amounts
        are live and reconcile to the statement below; treat the months as indicative. Two
        known simplifications: supplier bills are assumed payable this month (per-bill due
        dates come with the confirmed build), and the overheads figure is entered on this
        page, not yet a system setting.
    </Notice>

    @{
        // Computed ONCE per render and threaded through — the KPI strip, the table, the
        // chart and the reconciliation check must all describe the same forecast.
        var forecastView = TableReady && SelectedProjects.Count > 0 ? BuildForecast() : null;
    }

    @if (projectsFailed)
    {
        <p class="text-sm text-negative">Couldn't load the project list. The details are in the red bar at the top — reload to try again.</p>
    }
    else
    {
        <div class="mb-3 flex items-center justify-between gap-3 flex-wrap">
            @* The filter is a control, so it is never gated: it renders disabled with a
               loading label until the project list lands (the loading-states convention). *@
            @if (Projects.Current is null)
            {
                <select disabled
                        class="field">
                    <option>Loading projects…</option>
                </select>
            }
            else
            {
                <ProjectMultiSelect Projects="@(Projects.Current!)"
                                    Selected="@selectedIds"
                                    SelectedChanged="OnSelectionChangedAsync" />
            }
            @* Disabled while any selected row failed to load: the on-screen banner discloses
               the zeros, but a workbook travels without it — retry first, then export. *@
            <ExportToExcelButton FileName="Cash forecast"
                                 Disabled="@(!TableReady || SelectedProjects.Count == 0 || FailedSelectedProjects.Count > 0)"
                                 BuildWorkbook="BuildExportWorkbook" />
        </div>

        @* One gate for everything below: the forecast, the statement and the table are the
           same arithmetic over the same sources, so all of it reveals in one piece. *@
        <LoadGate IsLoading="@(!TableReady)" Prominent="true" Label="Loading project cashflows">
        @* The director's headline figures are the same forecast as the table below, so they wait
           inside the same gate: six tiles pulsing above a pulsing table is six marks too many. *@
        @if (IsDirector)
        {
            <ForecastKpiStrip Bank="BankSnapshot" BankReady="BankReady" TableReady="TableReady"
                              Forecast="forecastView" FetchedText="@FetchedText" />
        }
        @if (SelectedProjects.Count == 0)
        {
            <div class="panel p-8 text-center">
                <p class="text-sm text-content">No projects selected — pick some in the filter above.</p>
            </div>
        }
        else
        {
            @if (FailedSelectedProjects.Count > 0)
            {
                <Notice Tone="Tone.Negative" Class="mb-4">
                    The cash position could not be loaded for @string.Join(", ", FailedSelectedProjects.Select(p => p.Reference)) — those rows show zeros.
                    <button type="button" class="underline underline-offset-2 ml-1" @onclick="RetryFailedAsync">Retry</button>
                </Notice>
            }

            var forecast = forecastView!;

            <p class="text-xs text-content-subtle mb-3 max-w-4xl">
                <span class="font-medium text-content-muted">No signs to decode:</span> the band
                carries the direction — Cash in adds, Cash out subtracts; only Net movement and
                the balance are signed. Anything overdue sits in the current month, never the
                past. Flows with no honest date (no practical-completion date set) sit in
                Undated and never touch the balance.
            </p>

            <CashForecastTable Forecast="forecast" IsDirector="IsDirector"
                               ExpandedCategories="expandedCategories" OnToggle="ToggleCategory"
                               OnNextValuationChanged="args => OnNextValuationChangedAsync(args.Project, args.Args)"
                               OnMonthlyValuationChanged="args => OnMonthlyValuationChangedAsync(args.Project, args.Args)"
                               OverheadsDefault="overheadsMonthly" OverheadsOverrides="overheadsOverrides"
                               OnOverheadsDefaultChanged="OnOverheadsChanged"
                               OnOverheadsMonthChanged="args => OnOverheadMonthChangedAsync(args.Month, args.Args)" />

            @* ── The balance drawn: one line, the trough flagged. Directors only — it is the
               bank position over time. ── *@
            @if (IsDirector && forecast.Closing.Length > 1)
            {
                <div class="panel px-5 py-4 mb-6">
                    <p class="eyebrow mb-2">Closing bank balance by month</p>
                    <ForecastBalanceChart Axis="forecast.Axis" Closing="forecast.Closing"
                                          MinIndex="forecast.MinIndex"
                                          HighReference="@(BankSnapshot?.TotalCash ?? 0m)" />
                </div>
            }

            @* ── The promise, checked live: phased months per project must equal that
               project's completion cashflow on the statement below — to the penny. ── *@
            @if (forecast.Variances.Count == 0)
            {
                <p class="text-xs text-content-subtle mb-10 max-w-4xl">
                    <span class="text-positive font-semibold">✓ Ties to the statement below, to the penny.</span>
                    Every project's phased months (dated, later and undated together) sum to its
                    Project Completion Cashflow — the forecast is the same figures spread in
                    time, never a second opinion.
                </p>
            }
            else
            {
                <Notice Tone="Tone.Negative" Class="mb-10 max-w-4xl">
                    Phasing does not explain the statement for
                    @string.Join(", ", forecast.Variances.Select(variance => $"{variance.Project.Reference} ({Signed(variance.Variance)})"))
                    — this is a defect to report, not a judgement call. The statement below remains authoritative.
                </Notice>
            }

            @* ════════════════════════ the divider ════════════════════════ *@
            <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mb-6">
                <div class="h-px bg-line flex-1"></div>
                <p class="eyebrow whitespace-nowrap">Position to completion — the former Cash Summary</p>
                <div class="h-px bg-line flex-1"></div>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs text-content-subtle mb-4 max-w-4xl">
                The to-completion statement this page grew out of, unchanged: no time axis, just
                where the selected jobs' cash lands if they run to the end. The forecast above is
                these same figures with "when" added. The old bank tiles are not repeated here —
                their figures live in the strip at the top.
            </p>

            var combined = Totals();
            <CombinedStatementCard Combined="combined" SelectionLabel="@SelectionLabel" />
            <ProjectCashTable Rows="@(SelectedProjects.Select(project => (project, RowFor(project.ProjectId))).ToList())"
                              Total="combined" />
        }
        </LoadGate>
    }
</Page>
